  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "\\left|\\frac{D(n)}{n!} - \\frac{1}{e}\\right| \\leq \\frac{1}{(n+1)!},\\quad \\frac{D(n)}{n!} \\to \\frac{1}{e},\\quad \\text{with even partial sums above } \\frac{1}{e} \\text{ and odd partial sums below it.}",
    "plain": "Proves the sharp convergence rate |D(n)/n! - 1/e| ≤ 1/(n+1)! using the alternating series estimation theorem. The ratio D(n)/n! equals the n-th partial sum of the Taylor series for e^{-1}, and the error is bounded by the first omitted term. Also proves D(n)/n! → 1/e and the alternating property: even partial sums overshoot 1/e from above, odd partial sums undershoot from below.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "Connects the classical derangement ratio D(n)/n! with the Taylor expansion of e^{-1}.",
      "Records an explicit factorial error bound rather than only the limiting probability 1/e.",
      "Captures the alternating overshoot and undershoot behavior of the partial sums."
    ]
  },
