    "markdown": "# Knowledge Base: derangements-oq-03\n\nInsights accumulated during research on this problem.\n\n---\n\n## Problem Understanding\n\nThe local problem statement asks for the sharp convergence rate\n`|D(n)/n! - 1/e| <= 1/(n+1)!` for derangements. It frames `D(n)/n!` as the n-th partial sum of the Taylor series for `e^{-1}` and points to the alternating series estimation theorem as the intended proof mechanism.\n\n---\n\n## Insights\n\n- The main mathematical bridge is the equality between the derangement ratio and the finite alternating exponential sum.\n- The error bound should follow from bounding an alternating-series remainder by the first omitted term.\n- The target includes the parity behavior: even partial sums overshoot `1/e`, while odd partial sums undershoot it.\n\n---\n\n## Dead Ends\n\nNo failed approaches are recorded in the local research state.\n"
